course: physics
lesson: 43
title: Olympiad: sound, light, heat, electricity and magnets ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This block mixes the wobbles, rays and flows of the course, sound, light, heat, electricity and magnets, into puzzles where one sharp idea does all the work.
explain: Many puzzles hide a race: light arrives almost at once, while sound needs about three seconds for each kilometre, so the gap between the flash and the bang tells you how far away a storm is.
explain: Some puzzles hide a feeling that fools you: a metal railing feels colder than a wooden fence beside it, though both are at the same temperature, because metal draws heat out of your hand faster.
explain: Mirror puzzles need care: a word reads the same in a flat mirror only if it is spelt the same backwards and uses only letters that look the same flipped, such as A, H, I, M, O, T, U, V, W, X and Y.
explain: Picture Cerys in Cardiff in a storm: "It's chucking it down, mun, and the thunder's a million miles off": "chucking it down" means pouring with rain, "mun" means mate, and "a million miles" is hyperbole, so she should count the seconds instead.
fact: In air sound travels about one kilometre every three seconds, while light arrives almost instantly, so seconds divided by three gives the distance of a storm in kilometres.
fact: If the gaps between flash and thunder grow shorter, say 15, then 9, then 3 seconds, the storm is coming closer.
fact: TOOT, WOW and MOM read the same in a flat mirror, because they are spelt the same backwards and every letter looks the same flipped.
fact: A red apple under a blue lamp looks almost black, and a white T-shirt under a red lamp looks red, because white bounces back every colour that falls on it.
fact: Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach the Earth, so if the Sun went out we would still see it shining for about eight minutes.
fact: Light crosses empty space but sound cannot, so astronauts see the Sun but never hear it, and talk to each other by radio.
fact: A drink with ice in it stays near 0 degrees until all the ice has melted, because melting takes in heat.
fact: Two batteries in a row pushing the same way push about twice as hard; if one is turned round, they push against each other and the bulb hardly lights.
fact: In a string of lights with all the bulbs in one loop, one broken bulb stops the flow to all of them.
fact: A magnet snapped in two becomes two magnets, each with its own north and south pole.
fact: Iron, steel, nickel and cobalt are pulled strongly by a magnet; aluminium, copper and gold are not.
fact: Steam is invisible: the white cloud above a kettle is tiny droplets, and the real steam is in the clear gap just above the spout.
fact: Dirty snow melts faster than clean snow in the Sun, because the dark dirt soaks up sunlight while white snow bounces it away.
exercise: You see lightning and hear the thunder 12 seconds later. About how far away is the storm? | About 4 kilometres | About 12 kilometres | About 36 kilometres | About 1 kilometre
exercise: The gaps between flash and thunder are 15, then 9, then 3 seconds. What is the storm doing? | Coming closer to you | Moving away from you | Staying in one place | Getting weaker and dying out
exercise: Which word reads exactly the same in a flat mirror? | TOOT | BOOT | TOAD | STOP
exercise: Why is AMBULANCE written backwards on the front of an ambulance? | So drivers read it the right way in their mirrors | So people walking behind it can read it | Because that is how it is spelt in French | So that it glows more brightly at night
exercise: A red apple sits under a blue lamp. How does it look? | Almost black | Bright red | Bright blue | Pale pink with white spots
exercise: A white T-shirt is lit only by a red lamp. What colour does it look? | Red | White | Black | Blue
exercise: If the Sun suddenly went out, about how long would we still see it shining? | About 8 minutes | Not even a second | About 8 hours | About 8 whole days
exercise: On a frosty morning a metal railing and a wooden fence have both stood out all night. Which is colder? | Neither, they are just as cold as each other | The metal railing, as it feels colder | The wooden fence, as wood holds the frost | The metal one, as metal makes its own cold
exercise: A glass of juice full of ice sits in a warm kitchen. While there is still ice in it, the juice stays... | near 0 degrees | as warm as the kitchen | far below freezing, at -10 | a bit warmer every minute
exercise: A torch takes two batteries, and one is put in the wrong way round. What happens? | They push against each other and the bulb hardly lights | It shines twice as brightly | It shines as brightly as ever | The batteries recharge each other
exercise: In an old string of Christmas lights, all in one loop, one bulb breaks. What happens? | All the bulbs go out | Only that one bulb goes out | The other bulbs glow brighter | Only the bulbs next to it go out
exercise: A bar magnet is snapped in half. What do you get? | Two magnets, each with both poles | A north magnet and a south magnet | Two bits of steel that are not magnets any more | One magnet and one plain bit of steel
exercise: Which group could a magnet pick up completely? | A steel pin, an iron nail and a steel spoon | A steel pin, a copper coin and an iron nail | An aluminium can, an iron key and a steel pin | A gold ring, a steel spoon and an iron nail
exercise: Why can astronauts see the Sun but never hear it? | Light can cross empty space but sound cannot | The Sun is completely silent | Their helmets block every sound | The Sun's sounds are too high for us
exercise: Which is the odd one out in how it works: an echo, sonar, a bat's squeak, a rainbow? | A rainbow: it is not about sound | An echo: it is not about sound | Sonar: it is not about sound | A bat's squeak: it is not about sound
exercise: A kettle boils, with a white cloud above it. Where is the real steam? | In the clear gap just above the spout | In the thickest part of the white cloud | High up near the kitchen ceiling | Only inside the kettle, never outside
test: Cerys, in Cardiff: "It's chucking it down, mun, and the thunder's a million miles off." She counts 6 seconds after the flash. How far is the storm? | About 2 kilometres | A million miles away | About 6 kilometres | About 18 kilometres
test: "Counted 9 secs after the flash, no wait, 15." About how far is the storm? | About 5 kilometres | About 3 kilometres | About 9 kilometres | About 45 kilometres
test: Your bro swears he heard the lightning first and then saw the flash. Fair? | No, the light gets to you first | Yes, sound always travels faster than light | Yes, in heavy rain the sound overtakes it | No, the two always arrive at the same moment
test: In a cartoon, a spaceship blows up with a huge BOOM heard by heroes in another ship far away. Fix it. | They would see the blast but hear nothing | It would be even louder out in space | They would hear it but see nothing | They would hear it, but only a minute or so later
test: "Bruh, this metal bench is way colder than the wooden one, no cap." Both have been out all night. Put him right. | Both are equally cold; metal draws heat out of your hand faster | He's right, metal is always colder than wood | The wood is the colder one, it just hides it | Metal makes its own cold when it's dark
test: A Kiwi mum: "Run the jar under the hot tap, it'll open, sweet as." ("sweet as" means great.) Why does it work? | The metal lid grows more than the glass | The glass shrinks away from the lid as it warms | The hot water makes the lid slippery | The heat melts glue under the lid
test: Leo: "Fridge makes cold, so leave the door open and the kitchen cools down, ez." What's wrong? | A fridge only moves heat out of the box into the room | Nothing, the kitchen will soon get very cold | The fridge would freeze the whole kitchen | It works, but only in the middle of winter
test: Your compass swings the wrong way while you hold a steel torch right next to it. Why? | The steel pulls the needle harder than the Earth does | The torch beam pushes the needle round | Compasses only work in bright daylight | The batteries in the torch make the north pole move about
test: Riya, dusting the TV: "It's literally a dust magnet, I've wiped it a million times." What really pulls the dust on? | A little electric charge on the screen | A real magnet hidden inside the screen | The heat of the screen sucking it in | The light from the screen drawing it in
test: Put these in order, fastest first: light, sound in steel, sound in air. | Light, sound in steel, sound in air | Sound in steel, light, sound in air | Light, sound in air, sound in steel | Sound in air, sound in steel, light
test: Dirty snow and clean snow lie side by side in the sunshine. Which melts first? | The dirty snow | The clean snow | Both at once | Neither, ever
exam: Calculate the approximate distance of a storm whose thunder is heard 21 seconds after its lightning is seen. | About 7 kilometres | About 21 kilometres | About 3 kilometres | About 63 kilometres
exam: Identify the colour of a white garment illuminated solely by red light. | Red | White | Black | Green
exam: Determine the result of dividing a bar magnet into two parts. | Two magnets, each having both poles | Two separate single poles | Two pieces devoid of magnetism | One magnet and one inert piece
exam: Select the metal which a magnet does not attract. | Aluminium | Wrought iron | Steel | Nickel
